# FP-reasoning: MEDIUM, network_mode=tls-meta (works WITHOUT decryption — SNI is
# cleartext in the ClientHello). NON-payload TLS-metadata class (spec §22 C1/D6):
# decisive condition = SNI is a bare IPv4 literal. RFC 6066 forbids IP literals in
# SNI; legit clients always send a hostname -> an IP-literal SNI is attacker-shaped
# (scanners, hardcoded-IP C2, misconfigured tooling). bounded anchored pcre -> no
# backtracking (§19 D5). Residual FP (rare internal IP-SNI tooling) -> tune via
# threshold.config (NOT deprecation). Negative: a normal hostname SNI exercises the
# same tls.sni decisive surface but fails the IP regex -> silent. IPv6-literal SNI
# = roadmap companion rule.
alert tls any any -> any any (msg:"TLS anomaly - SNI is a bare IPv4 literal (non-RFC6066, suspicious client) [medium]"; flow:established,to_server; tls.sni; pcre:"/^\d{1,3}(?:\.\d{1,3}){3}$/"; classtype:bad-unknown; metadata: asset_category tls, owasp none, attack_target Server, mitre_tactic_id TA0011, mitre_tactic_name Command-and-Control, mitre_technique_id T1071.001, mitre_technique_name Application-Layer-Protocol-Web, kill_chain C2, confidence medium, signature_severity Major, deployment Perimeter, network_mode tls-meta, cve none, poc_ref tests/poc/9400001_sni-bare-ip/poc.sh, tag tls-sni-anomaly, created_at 2026_05_18, updated_at 2026_05_18, author huntingyuk; sid:9400001; rev:1;)
# FP-reasoning: MEDIUM, network_mode=tls-meta (no decryption — JA3 is computed
# from the cleartext ClientHello). JA3 is a BEHAVIOURAL fingerprint, not an IP
# (allowed). Low-half-life (spec §22 D6): dataset entries carry provenance + a
# review/expire date (see rules/datasets/badja3.lst) and the rule is paired with a
# non-fingerprint qualifier -- SNI must be present (bsize:>0) -- so a single
# randomised-JA3 collision on a SNI-less raw probe does not alert. Decisive
# condition = ja3.hash IN the curated bad-list. Negative: a ClientHello with a
# DIFFERENT JA3 (not in the list) exercises the same ja3.hash path -> silent.
alert tls any any -> any any (msg:"TLS anomaly - client JA3 on curated bad-list [medium]"; flow:established,to_server; ja3.hash; dataset:isset,badja3, type string, load datasets/badja3.lst; tls.sni; bsize:>0; classtype:bad-unknown; metadata: asset_category tls, owasp none, attack_target Server, mitre_tactic_id TA0011, mitre_tactic_name Command-and-Control, mitre_technique_id T1071.001, mitre_technique_name Application-Layer-Protocol-Web, kill_chain C2, confidence medium, signature_severity Major, deployment Perimeter, network_mode tls-meta, cve none, poc_ref tests/poc/9400002_ja3-badlist/poc.sh, tag tls-ja3-badlist, created_at 2026_05_18, updated_at 2026_05_18, author huntingyuk; sid:9400002; rev:1;)
# FP-reasoning: MEDIUM, network_mode=tls-meta (cert is sent cleartext in the TLS
# handshake; no decryption needed). NON-payload TLS-metadata class. Decisive
# condition = the presented SERVER certificate is past its notAfter
# (tls_cert_expired, engine-evaluated). An expired server cert is an abandoned-infra
# / interception / sloppy-C2 indicator. Residual FP = a real service running an
# expired cert -> suppress that dst via threshold.config (NOT deprecation). Negative
# exercises the SAME cert-parse path with a VALID self-signed cert -> not expired
# -> silent.
alert tls any any -> any any (msg:"TLS anomaly - expired server certificate presented [medium]"; flow:established; tls_cert_expired; classtype:bad-unknown; metadata: asset_category tls, owasp none, attack_target Server, mitre_tactic_id TA0011, mitre_tactic_name Command-and-Control, mitre_technique_id T1071.001, mitre_technique_name Application-Layer-Protocol-Web, kill_chain C2, confidence medium, signature_severity Major, deployment Perimeter, network_mode tls-meta, cve none, poc_ref tests/poc/9400003_expired-cert/poc.sh, tag tls-cert-expired, created_at 2026_05_18, updated_at 2026_05_18, author huntingyuk; sid:9400003; rev:1;)
